<http://data.silknow.org/object/b64bef77-be2b-3201-a207-3e6a04057933/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "The earliest samplers were reference works for embroiderers. They showed 'samples' of patterns and stitches and recorded how to achieve particular effects. In Europe in the 17th century samplers provided instruction and practice for girls learning needlework. From the 1750s, makers developed a type of sampler in which they demonstrated their darning skills.  The anonymous embroiderer of this sampler probably did not have to learn to darn in order to earn a living. She has used a variety of pastel-coloured silks and filled the centre of the sampler with a delicate ribbon-tied spray of flowers. This is a decorative sampler suitable for display."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/b64bef77-be2b-3201-a207-3e6a04057933> .

